    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine when the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined, we need to find the values of \\(y\\) that make the denominator zero. The denominator of the expression is \\(y^2 - 5y + 4\\). We set the denominator equal to zero and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny^2 - 5y + 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, which we can factorize as follows:\n\n\\[\ny^2 - 5y + 4 = (y - 1)(y - 4) = 0\n\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[\ny - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y - 4 = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = 4\n\\]\n\nThese values of \\(y\\) make the denominator zero, and thus the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined. The sum of all values of \\(y\\) that make the expression undefined is:\n\n\\[\n1 + 4 = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all values of \\(y\\) for which the expression is undefined is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
